# flabel's `threshold.config` — deliberately EMPTY, and that is the whole point.
#
# This is Suricata's suppression and rate-limiting file: entries here silently drop or throttle
# alerts. Without this file the engine falls back to `/etc/suricata/threshold.config`, a
# package-managed file on the labelling machine that `config_sha256` does not cover — so anyone
# with root on that box, or a future package revision, could remove labels from the corpus with
# nothing in the run block recording that it happened.
#
# Measured 2026-08-28: `tools/flabel-run` invokes flabel under sudo, so Suricata runs as root and
# the fallback file's 0750 permissions do not isolate anything. A directory mode cannot protect a
# process running as root; owning the path can.
#
# Never add a suppression here without saying why in the commit. A label that is silently absent is
# indistinguishable from a flow that was never malicious, which is the one thing the store cannot
# represent (spec §13).
